 :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437855}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437854} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d01df11-c07e-5e3b-a901-c578fe72ec74" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9278ec55-4908-5c1c-8a74-640611473537" :question/math-academy-id "q-131196" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4bf8de7e8719545b3d8abf5dc9fb4bc0c5f71034dba7186b7d9822d9e8be044d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\n- **II.** $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\n- **III.** $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\n- **IV.** $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement III is true, whereas statements I and II are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $x$-components decrease as we move horizontally towards the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "c4ea5422-7fe4-544c-96ca-1a8f6b528092"}] :activity/title "The Divergence of a Vector Field" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436920}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483488} :activity/id #uuid "13de39d3-3bb8-598c-9ef9-38c2d90d7a15" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049459 :topic/id #uuid "f6fd4776-56f0-5112-9cdc-83e344004d93" :topic/math-academy-id 2131} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483433 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14e8ce4b-4397-5131-8016-ac24d8139492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482848} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0cb281-c41d-56fc-b840-37c1e1e299b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482947} {:answer/id #uuid "86a9b14f-682a-56ff-b5d7-630ab6d0e48f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483046} {:answer/id #uuid "8c82e88d-36a4-5cf5-b6fa-804eb0316710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483060} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ee5433-5dac-5d77-ba1c-0052b43bbfb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483177}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483060} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb66c9a3-57be-589b-8044-dbc471e3b934" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "aefd685f-6837-5195-a54c-56ec619b88d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-105574" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c1dd3e659d2580103232fa68620c60f493477649e1c4a8880023e6c6de88a167.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\nIII. $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\nIV. $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement III is true, whereas statements I and II are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components decrease as we move vertically towards the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "05100288-b9ed-59fd-b7a9-9a2592fbbb50" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483450}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483443 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266056 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10bcdd62-0b56-5da2-a50a-586faa6696b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy{z}^{2}+xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263155} {:answer/id #uuid "295ca0f4-57e7-5a42-ac1c-c9565fd958a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y{z}^{2}+xy{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263355} {:answer/id #uuid "2be15acb-dbc4-5b00-98a8-38e26089e1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5y{z}^{2}+x{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263371} {:answer/id #uuid "40fe9bd6-26ef-51ba-a8ee-4aa3a8d37085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y{z}^{2}+xy+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263576} {:answer/id #uuid "fe783955-ec8b-51ba-88fd-5f0cc372a593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+{e}^{y}+{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265240}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263371} :answer-field/id #uuid "6647713f-61bd-5d5d-9904-3d926c79511c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "01f7efc1-008b-5b39-b852-37db53b31085" :question/math-academy-id "q-131176" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=2xy{z}^{2}\\,i+x{e}^{y}\\,j+y{z}^{3}\\,k.$ \nFind $∇\\cdot F.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =2y{z}^{2}+x{e}^{y}+3y{z}^{2} \\\\  & =5y{z}^{2}+x{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1b54e950-07e6-508f-8bbf-26f7181e85f0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483450 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71cbe9b9-abfb-59ee-9a3b-f7f689c26688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257781} {:answer/id #uuid "88b7f1be-ad85-5371-a6af-84faa05bd3aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257981} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c60dec-38f0-52c7-b0aa-6a4369234d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258609} {:answer/id #uuid "d40a47e0-a307-5498-8db8-91105ba44fa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258639} {:answer/id #uuid "edb9593d-e1cc-5ea7-ae77-b57065d0d67e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258838}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258639} :answer-field/id #uuid "31c01b0b-506b-5b5b-95a1-7bc8278302dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "74e7bf88-8da6-528c-84d5-ded22deb1ee4" :question/math-academy-id "q-123200" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=(x-y)^{3}\\,i+(y+z)^{3}\\,j+(x+z)^{3}\\,k.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y+z)^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x+z)^{3}) \\\\  & =3(x-y)^{2}+3(y+z)^{2}+3(x+z)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,2,2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(-2,2,2) & =3((-2)-(2))^{2}+3((2)+(2))^{2}+3((-2)+(2))^{2} \\\\  & =3(-4)^{2}+3(4)^{2}+3(0)^{2} \\\\  & =96.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2e0d18ce-acf0-5440-b4c0-fe1c45eb2401" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483443}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483483 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43bc396a-d9ed-5bb3-b93d-0fb796ec2097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482927} {:answer/id #uuid "516c5078-1161-5f0b-8b9d-0636463d00b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482956} {:answer/id #uuid "7b6cb7f0-12ce-523b-a991-3f45078b3156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483028} {:answer/id #uuid "87d47221-a635-52a3-99d0-f5a56e12e0ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483050} {:answer/id #uuid "94b6c747-e673-5cf6-a43f-ad3df20d8406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483071}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483028} :answer-field/id #uuid "22420a9a-1575-5698-aa34-04729efa2a61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3aad6c37-ff96-5a6c-89b9-a9f32a30b3d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-105570" :question/problem "![](images/2b/2b64c156519b685acf390c51c1ea3e1540d82e0f7e047cf2f804d38a23956604.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\nIII. $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\nIV. $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the length of the vectors' $x$-components increase as we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "9e3f811c-54a6-5c26-bdfa-8aaa897c63e3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483433}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a8a0905-3f5a-57f9-b71d-c2127323b463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482943} {:answer/id #uuid "5b08ebea-7284-5412-99de-e04b22ab6d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4+\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482971} {:answer/id #uuid "5db37158-5959-5928-a4a3-8612494e2e92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482973} {:answer/id #uuid "7a1781db-53be-5369-8346-96df148295ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483025} {:answer/id #uuid "d185a6d1-cc28-52e1-8c76-58d00373669d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3+\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483162}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483162} :answer-field/id #uuid "346d1a8e-e297-5ff1-8dff-7d5740531d20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "75ca7d55-6e3f-58c8-8a50-805ce5d1a756" :question/math-academy-id "q-41838" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F={x}^{2}yz\\,i+{x}^{3}y\\,j+z\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})\\,k.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-1,-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})) \\\\  & =2xyz+{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-1,-1,2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(-1,-1,2) & =2(-1)(-1)(2)+(-1)^{3}+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{-1}) \\\\  & =4-1+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "adea8609-2822-5adc-b454-084b01390c94" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483483}}] :activity/title "The Divergence of a Vector Field" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482799}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134753} :activity/id #uuid "cb02e51b-dee7-54a8-8f76-914d0b42e66b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997 :topic/id #uuid "24e61144-3626-5d23-b2e6-a33ee94f32f3" :topic/math-academy-id 2151} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134753 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135082 :tutorial/content "How do we represent the double integral of a function over a non-rectangular region as a repeated integral? \n\nSpecifically, let's consider\n![](images/3a/3ac99dd8336c3a877fbf6ba63a3074a490b85c8a350adad00da423412dd60ee3.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg)  is the finite region enclosed by the lines ![](images/df/df9822243c11ec9f0fabca5cc578976d0bdea2c521b4f61964f5f3850a970504.svg), ![](images/1f/1fb9f2f73a09599931892c1dee7629975b4d851e5dd6f0d07b7c641bbf17bf54.svg), ![](images/23/238e977be23f692486c8bf68f6b40255435b3d1796cc50d260a014a8c670be7b.svg) and ![](images/b5/b5489c125683eb29c2cababe2e1b7094d6a16b89d09970c42af649df70d045e4.svg) as shown below.\n\n![](images/af/af0a1a74df8aec0d5189dc2402fc1840c25d4cd94d8861fa06110cae7b449843.png)\n\nFirst, notice that ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is a *type I* region. \nTo determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/39/39629b33e25aed05bf9a5ca36180f984e7ff8f863c6966d47f60483d672a8af4.png)\n\nSo, the region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) can be defined as \n\n![](images/78/78cf2ea7f960497f7cc9beea602bf6ca0e82b74491f9f56cce76130d965d4a64.svg)\n\nNow, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n![](images/3c/3c423b0d423203621ab89258a6fff4cea190279b4dcc7170c8a7070bf1f85e24.svg)\n\nAnd that's our answer! Notice that, when we have a type I region, \n\n- the limits for ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) are functions of ![](images/5a/5aac2c81021f3f9e468e7840c5f95ece35c10b894bdae19ec1d247bdeb47ac6f.svg)\n\n- the limits for ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) are constants, and\n\n- we integrate over the variable ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) before integrating over the variable ![](images/cf/cff7b257e3c71ec70dc7cd273bdd1cced4254993a43cffbfc338460c21bc7319.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "7c641021-bc5e-5b05-8a23-b198735ee5a3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4261 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4a5c014d-2a5f-5ef2-b451-9d6636c3241e" :step/math-academy-id 25905 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134754}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134754 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133833 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133648 :question/id #uuid "51467bae-f181-53ba-adf2-1218dc16275f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7024"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e891363-fea2-5de4-9bbb-67fddb077200" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050822}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445829}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given"} :step/id #uuid "3edf61a9-ab06-533a-ad5f-906f95245bb3" :step/math-academy-id 25906 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134755}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134755 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133834 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133649 :question/id #uuid "7f443df5-1c26-5af4-9541-e83522126197" :question/math-academy-id "e-7025"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853b2770-5057-50aa-9c15-7426cf4cc6a4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483386}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"} :step/id #uuid "621aac0e-49f3-510c-b684-d0f438398280" :step/math-academy-id 25907 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134756}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134756 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135083 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to evaluate the repeated integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{x}^{2x} (x-2y) \\ \\mathrm{d}  y \\ \\mathrm{d}  x.\n$$\n\nNotice that the domain of integration is a type I region. \n\nTo compute this integral, we must bear the following important points in mind.\n\n- We start with the inner integral, as usual.\n\n- When integrating with respect to $y,$ we treat $x$ as though it were a constant.\n\n- The limits of integration for the inner integral are functions of $x.$ Once again, we treat $x$ as though it were a constant and substitute the integration limits as usual. After this step, there should be no more $y$'s in our integral.\n\n- Finally, we compute the outer integral.\n\nLet's apply these steps to solve the given problem.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{x}^{2x}(x-2y)\\text{ }dy]\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[xy-{y}^{2}]_{x}^{2x}\\text{ }dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[x\\cdot (2x)-{(2x)}^{2}]-[x\\cdot (x)-(x)^{2}]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[2{x}^{2}-4{x}^{2}]-[{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-2{x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,dx & =-2{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{[{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}[{1}^{3}-{0}^{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3698e7e3-4bc2-5db0-96b5-172131284b4a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4262 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Repeated Integrals With Variable Limits"} :step/id #uuid "59bc86e8-4dae-564b-ab2b-c8ac5309d3be" :step/math-academy-id 25908 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134757}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134757 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133835 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133650 :question/id #uuid "b151be22-91a1-5303-b86d-4f9ba748f000" :question/math-academy-id "e-7026"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a28c4320-e2dd-5195-81ac-b910c2328691" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050207}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483378}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type I Region"} :step/id #uuid "9095d8fd-3ce2-5af6-bda4-4a49a820addf" :step/math-academy-id 25909 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134758}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134758 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133836 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133651 :question/id #uuid "24dd94c8-2821-5614-9ccf-43c3656267f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7027"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69f6b2a7-7a3a-5c2c-90db-5aa321784048" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483407}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type I Region"} :step/id #uuid "bb2a0a7b-340c-53a3-a510-9a559cc6d898" :step/math-academy-id 25911}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Type I Regions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134020}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408133} :activity/id #uuid "74e0d3a9-0075-5d33-b25c-7e801fde9553" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997 :topic/id #uuid "24e61144-3626-5d23-b2e6-a33ee94f32f3" :topic/math-academy-id 2151} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408133 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d2a009-9a40-5d25-99e2-4f97af963231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321192} {:answer/id #uuid "2c533ee8-f9db-5f4a-a560-8035da471b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321490} {:answer/id #uuid "3a0552d2-cfa6-5c1e-901c-be3cad62fa64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321590} {:answer/id #uuid "70ec84ef-6655-53bf-9eb0-7897a3753db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322030} {:answer/id #uuid "80d50f5a-f000-539b-a1db-5c8a13952505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322160}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322160} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b2db87a-5385-53da-8e27-89a6fe772ff2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "39356ac1-0a31-5e6e-9a6f-6a96bbbbe55c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37683" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}y\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{x}^{1/x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)\\,dx & =\\frac{1}{2}{[-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}}-x]}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{1}{3}+1-(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{1}{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{11}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "098028bf-3706-5127-9bb7-debd78e7b253" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408136}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408134 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "100fac5f-cc12-56dc-9567-ee01010ba781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408119} {:answer/id #uuid "1377aca7-a017-58fe-8e23-256c042e11b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408120} {:answer/id #uuid "3aabdbf2-aad9-5028-8b69-34e437771408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408121} {:answer/id #uuid "41ea5617-4c16-5bdc-a231-c9432f86dc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408122} {:answer/id #uuid "93d7f804-0816-5794-84e7-a2fe566f8c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408127}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408121} :answer-field/id #uuid "85ef2574-1c06-549a-a456-4b22b82491c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ffe5914e-4912-5ff3-9b15-3a1462960003" :question/math-academy-id "q-132285" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy\\,dx=$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}]_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+1-{x}^{4}-{x}^{4})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{4})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{4})\\,dx & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x-\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+1-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "20fb188a-faf6-5a84-b51f-1b3b2dd89239"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408135 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a7ddc79-cbfd-59b5-9638-d178b71104a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348536} {:answer/id #uuid "4b79160f-37ab-5192-8514-437d9b279188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349138} {:answer/id #uuid "544819eb-b6d1-5a40-bb3a-d34d78eef696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349218} {:answer/id #uuid "a06fce6a-f06f-595a-9863-76102c8d8305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349871} {:answer/id #uuid "f06aa1a1-4329-543e-8ec1-01bf70f9f867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350550}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349871} :answer-field/id #uuid "08077c01-ccff-5e05-a424-ef1243bb7696" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b8f9d362-d296-527d-b3eb-8711496d6537" :question/math-academy-id "q-81367" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dA,$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines\n$x=e,$ $y=e$ and the curve $y={e}^{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/aa/aa635ca5ef3198fea56d684b034de476a72e4ace2a4c51b37c6c59977036eb76.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- The line $y=e$ intercepts the curve $y={e}^{x}$ at $x=1.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$1\\le x\\le e.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/b7/b78021a06c9fea100b75c66a619c425345cda3666d84da018895be15a53af06d.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{x}\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dA={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}[{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)]}_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx & ={[\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{2}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡e}{2}-\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "597be508-6945-5d32-9627-435849b9418f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408134}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408136 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323953 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b726bfa-ba4c-5d91-9f6d-6a3764d8755d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321242} {:answer/id #uuid "1594e17d-7011-5f7d-aa6a-12336ec25a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321322} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5f94c5-0d1b-5f79-944c-8cb9d821beed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321479} {:answer/id #uuid "564621d9-f224-5fff-b159-cf3615e51a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321828} {:answer/id #uuid "d4f698ad-bda0-5b89-8175-c64721d84bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322805}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321479} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eb45d35-7041-5bf4-8524-44adec58ddbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ad4a8e76-c297-5cff-8131-747df131b332" :question/math-academy-id "q-37865" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dA,$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the line\n$y=1-x$ and the coordinate axes." :question/worked-solution "Let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/95/95ebd0a4eb874972b459e53eecf1d57650f63284698fcad967c09620ac01cd99.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- The line $y=1-x$ intercepts the $x$-axis at $x=1.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/d0/d048f20f2299722cee598cc1005237627ecdd7643e996b269f26b5a096f01f2f.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{-x+1}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{1}{1+x+y}]}_{0}^{-x+1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{1+x+(1-x)}-(-\\frac{1}{1+x+0}))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{1+x})dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{1+x})dx & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-(0+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "780a6e09-0224-5a5e-9ae0-33ed21780a56" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408137}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408137 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58f2f8c3-eca9-558a-ae7f-9a78208457ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{-{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408123} {:answer/id #uuid "647e13d7-fe24-5171-9f25-59459873bf42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408124} {:answer/id #uuid "93175eb3-2514-5cc0-a423-e088c96ac28d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{-{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408125} {:answer/id #uuid "93b1f2e7-cf7b-5b8a-b5bb-c62e89e19fa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{-{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408126} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3be0a4-83b9-54e6-81b8-88c21e425fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{-1}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408128}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408126} :answer-field/id #uuid "3df51106-2408-5663-8451-c957471724da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3676ec52-02c6-5b83-a547-e552c5ceb042" :question/math-academy-id "q-81338" :question/problem "![](images/41/4121d975786de30f34e493e7ac49e5cdc8e66621e800321008ba4ba501fb818f.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=-{x}^{2},$ and $y=-1,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$-1\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/ef/ef6450f795d0edea8a3217e28d7f1a966d5b1c62f9241826213de0485af5f860.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le -{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{-{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c5dbc16c-f16e-55e5-b468-49e1c21fdfb0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408135}}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Type I Regions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408118}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482654} :activity/id #uuid "d50c6341-afab-531b-95d7-80980e2f8edd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997 :topic/id #uuid "24e61144-3626-5d23-b2e6-a33ee94f32f3" :topic/math-academy-id 2151} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482622 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a55a9b55-701b-54b8-9dc3-ff767eb9a16b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267638} {:answer/id #uuid "b89fe4b4-ca5f-5b19-9c79-5f2ac46d2595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267800} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6a5ad1-907d-59c7-a9c8-ed050fde1508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267859} {:answer/id #uuid "e85875be-4289-5cdd-8c4f-d83917f81081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268207} {:answer/id #uuid "fcfb32be-1877-5c91-86e5-ee84568b6b69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268375}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268375} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bb0802c-c72d-5b84-a668-8e4527960b23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "07938b36-7b86-5365-9a53-980eadb141d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-132276" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dy\\,dx=$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x[\\,y\\,]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,dx & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-2) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "09dc735b-be54-5f55-a40c-b3de9cb3c25f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482646 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351369 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "125b9147-6355-50f2-ba8c-748686fc87f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348607} {:answer/id #uuid "6df205c3-6135-5d2b-b3fd-a8bcf211e047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349456} {:answer/id #uuid "8a2bfe7d-c973-5e74-8dfd-79e2236afc46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349700} {:answer/id #uuid "90178deb-05ed-554a-b6da-bcd3010a2bf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349741} {:answer/id #uuid "f59a2f53-d71c-5744-ba75-d029ceccffcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350594}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350594} :answer-field/id #uuid "c92c3f4e-98f4-5231-9b54-c6264a5301cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "adc09746-e915-53ae-9b39-14d8d6a2a18e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81774" :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{D}x\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,2{x}^{2}\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+1\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over the type I region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,2{x}^{2}\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+1\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}+1}x\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}+1}x\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x[{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}+1}dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x[y]_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}+1}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x({x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(1-{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{3})dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{3})dx & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b1d9c3ed-33de-5684-8d99-d7f5f9b0c7e9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482654 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "357e7017-c759-53cd-bd18-3b9cac8a059e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{x+1}^{3-{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348931} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb83aea-26c4-5042-8913-2833d34004fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}[{\\int }_{x+1}^{3-{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349582} {:answer/id #uuid "89dc3879-a9e4-5291-a430-52520db7f2af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}[{\\int }_{3-{x}^{2}}^{x+1}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349697} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f30da7-1720-596a-b960-80e3393eccfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{1}[{\\int }_{x+1}^{3-{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350448} {:answer/id #uuid "f54b2107-78b3-5b8f-b15e-e4e04bce393b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{1}[{\\int }_{3-{x}^{2}}^{x+1}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350592}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350448} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e294541-5f38-5092-8793-4eff75d982fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a289df3d-5c34-5bee-85db-98d71fbc43da" :question/math-academy-id "q-81340" :question/problem "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=3-{x}^{2}$ and $y=x+1,$ then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/73/7316d667d7308ac5ec0f5348055d3d0e7eb90b97abaa74f5693c3151dc3b8bb1.png)\n\nNow, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-{x}^{2} & =x+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$-2\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/aa/aab6fb99694b518bfa9d200a75876c9e888ba1ab7d699464bc9caca360be775a.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 1,\\,x+1\\le y\\le 3-{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{x+1}^{3-{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}[{\\int }_{x+1}^{3-{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e0bceb01-13f0-500a-a70e-d7c56dc36189" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ba8975f-1dcf-5982-82b5-4619f25392af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349440} {:answer/id #uuid "909c8869-f444-52b1-8e81-f3f7d8cd7ea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349746} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd6c44c-c13b-51de-a23a-1e56014ae0e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349844} {:answer/id #uuid "a3dbc989-fd06-546f-a58e-5a71b4609f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349910} {:answer/id #uuid "d230d5cd-76aa-5b60-afae-1d4ccdb5bb52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{41}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350291}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349844} :answer-field/id #uuid "cef03a78-3898-53d7-a31f-076237c237e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bba1a6f7-479e-5c7c-a4a7-2bc8097d537b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81361" :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{D}3x{e}^{y}\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over the type I region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3x{e}^{y}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}3x{e}^{y}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}3x{e}^{y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{e}^{y}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x{[{e}^{y}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}-{e}^{0})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x(x-1)dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(3{x}^{2}-3x)dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(3{x}^{2}-3x)dx & ={[{x}^{3}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(64-24)-(1-\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =40+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ed3c1020-e19e-5ebc-82db-39b373afe65a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482646}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482544 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eac9096-a6ad-5d5e-9517-5c0ac0728af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-x/2}^{5-{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481809} {:answer/id #uuid "533ce4cc-1308-5319-806a-167ee6b29c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-x/2}^{0}{\\int }_{-2}^{5-{x}^{2}}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481974} {:answer/id #uuid "a522044d-f1ef-55e8-b1ee-f64410c9b0e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{5-{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{-x/2}^{0}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482166} {:answer/id #uuid "c7a488ab-7038-5a44-bd73-39ae0126a903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{5-{x}^{2}}^{-x/2}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482250} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f6ba16-5e4c-5134-9ee4-51804e3e92c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-x/2}^{5-{x}^{2}}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482251}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482251} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dce35b7-572a-54b4-af44-152ecca7b2e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "61556a8f-0a92-5ecd-8c20-5948347f1756" :question/math-academy-id "q-132221" :question/problem "![](images/a6/a68077b18f8c08fc7cd68889b6472373c7677bcfcb6f370abce1e93cf67c4594.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the line $x=0,$ and the curves $y=5-{x}^{2}$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}x & =5-{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}x-5 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-x-10 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(2x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard the solution $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ since $x\\le 0$ in our region. Therefore, $x$-limits are $-2\\le x\\le 0.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/5b/5bafe1d8cd35eec83aba3a9a24f777340a09f824919d4cedc5879c774e30cf5e.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}x\\le y\\le 5-{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-x/2}^{5-{x}^{2}}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f943e97a-19e3-5eb0-b644-0fd2bcdae89f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482622}}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Type I Regions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481771}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483434} :activity/id #uuid "61b909f0-ec60-5577-af75-e7859906eb7e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997 :topic/id #uuid "24e61144-3626-5d23-b2e6-a33ee94f32f3" :topic/math-academy-id 2151} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483434 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483386 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f2a6974-156a-58a1-912d-d4e9e5c9106b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{1}[{\\int }_{1-{x}^{2}}^{x-1}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482893} {:answer/id #uuid "2fd070a6-0119-5dfa-9699-94cd22f9107e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{1}[{\\int }_{x-1}^{1-{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482895} {:answer/id #uuid "69d1e9a7-9e40-528b-9934-3556f8295863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{x-1}^{1-{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482995} {:answer/id #uuid "90fcc974-e6d1-5c13-933d-0f0e398bfcdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{x-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{-2}^{1-{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483066} {:answer/id #uuid "c039e0dd-3463-5163-9811-98a986c4da00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{1-{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{-2}^{1-{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483135}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482895} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b82a6ed-9537-5fe1-9550-c6deec72fd33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "888487e0-300d-510a-97fe-31196306b818" :question/math-academy-id "q-81344" :question/problem "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=1-{x}^{2}$ and $y=x-1,$ then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/24/24683f81da1c7e90ccbd51748f5f46407137be4a40ef5f0678292b70f0781eff.png)\n\nNow, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2} & =x-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$-2\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/e9/e955ad94bca70a8e6f996d81814ffa8fcc7a322d5979db7b0726d39558b6aa86.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 1,\\,x-1\\le y\\le 1-{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{1-{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}[{\\int }_{x-1}^{1-{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0ba397eb-27d1-5f99-9aee-673204cdeaff" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483479}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483453 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268855 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "576ddeae-8148-5d98-9276-c4916d082cfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266923} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3026f4-d919-504f-ae3a-79b5af8301a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267406} {:answer/id #uuid "c93138df-b030-5c7f-b21f-415d12704304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267948} {:answer/id #uuid "e2affe66-cebd-58ac-b5cd-9043cda4b543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268147} {:answer/id #uuid "ed66d9ac-44b3-55cb-8a98-dbe724a419de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268251}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267406} :answer-field/id #uuid "eba13f08-f419-5a9a-9957-7c081d134163" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "63aade32-c436-5c43-9ff2-73d45e2de0f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-132282" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,dy\\,dx=$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4x\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4x\\,dx & ={[2{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2{(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3af15913-e8bd-5162-bb11-0248e7dc9e39"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483479 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ba8975f-1dcf-5982-82b5-4619f25392af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349440} {:answer/id #uuid "909c8869-f444-52b1-8e81-f3f7d8cd7ea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349746} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd6c44c-c13b-51de-a23a-1e56014ae0e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349844} {:answer/id #uuid "a3dbc989-fd06-546f-a58e-5a71b4609f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349910} {:answer/id #uuid "d230d5cd-76aa-5b60-afae-1d4ccdb5bb52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{41}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350291}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349844} :answer-field/id #uuid "cef03a78-3898-53d7-a31f-076237c237e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bba1a6f7-479e-5c7c-a4a7-2bc8097d537b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81361" :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{D}3x{e}^{y}\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over the type I region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3x{e}^{y}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}3x{e}^{y}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}3x{e}^{y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{e}^{y}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x{[{e}^{y}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}-{e}^{0})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x(x-1)dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(3{x}^{2}-3x)dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(3{x}^{2}-3x)dx & ={[{x}^{3}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(64-24)-(1-\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =40+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8054baf7-76fc-513c-bc8c-95f9b72f74a0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483480}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483480 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483407 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "158bc465-6c57-523d-9cf3-6f83f39945fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482851} {:answer/id #uuid "1b905570-3bc1-56c6-b7bf-ad922d213480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482867} {:answer/id #uuid "35ff924d-fce0-5c71-95a8-6294d9e42e37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482905} {:answer/id #uuid "6f0180ea-f116-5dcb-8c89-f5ec1242588d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483002} {:answer/id #uuid "f32f6e2b-ace4-5f4f-b08b-21b50a871738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483222}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482905} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d527e9c-2e5b-5948-a599-77a3b28decc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c45bffe5-25bf-5869-8e87-fa24cb3ec218" :question/math-academy-id "q-81369" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}8x{y}^{3}\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and below the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Since $y\\le 0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\\,⟹\\,y=-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/9c/9c6b1dd7d81d124888ddd588027eed908a0eaecb2c42ebc79f3253c4123dbe86.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- We already know the intersection of the circle with the line $y=0.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$-1\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/bd/bd584d7481549b792e786a7186ba7ee87cd536258c5a0135f36ee17d96c4880d.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}8x{y}^{3}\\,dA={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{0}8x{y}^{3}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{0}8x{y}^{3}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}2x[{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{0}4{y}^{3}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}2x{[{y}^{4}]}_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{0}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}2x(0-{(-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}})}^{4})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-2x(1-{x}^{2})^{2})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-2x(1-{x}^{2})^{2})\\,dx & ={[\\frac{(1-{x}^{2})^{3}}{3}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(1-{1}^{2})^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(1-(-1)^{2})^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "85ca4b6f-ad35-500c-8f70-cb09fc202e4f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483501}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483501 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483378 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12c2c52e-7edb-55fd-994f-8e6f5b4d4dd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482845} {:answer/id #uuid "15e4ed08-ffaf-52de-97e2-cfa7db153d54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482853} {:answer/id #uuid "31357fe9-e1fa-537d-89ee-79b4cac4b7cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482899} {:answer/id #uuid "4d8fcc2a-87cb-52dc-883a-bec15bf93188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482951} {:answer/id #uuid "60d03f26-4c90-5b4e-b801-3e8d67f98fb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482981}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482899} :answer-field/id #uuid "09e3c1e0-e6e5-5167-83b3-cae11270cfdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "790fc9b6-72db-52a7-bf88-41ac2c2d70f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-132281" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\text{ }x(y-1)\\,dy\\,dx=$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\text{ }x(y-1)\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x[{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\text{ }(y-1)\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-y]}_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(\\frac{1}{2}(1-{x}^{2})-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}(1-{x}^{2})-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(-2\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-2x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}-2x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\,dx & ={[\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})^{3/2}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dc3dd7a9-c45b-51da-a49f-0da3682d6427" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483453}}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Type I Regions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482806}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130016} :activity/id #uuid "e669e123-e445-550d-980b-09ee4b440f81" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050045 :topic/id #uuid "28837c1e-d226-56e8-b958-b4375cfa8078" :topic/math-academy-id 1978} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130016 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130370 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following function: \n\n$$\nw = f(x,y,z) =x^2 +y^2+z^2\n$$\n\nSadly, it is impossible to plot this function because four spatial dimensions ($x,y,z,$ and $w$) are needed, and space as we know it is restricted to three dimensions. \n\nHowever, we can get an idea of how the function $f(x,y,z)$ behaves by plotting surfaces of the form\n\n$$\n\\left\\{(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3 \\, : \\,f(x,y,z)=c\\right\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is some value that is in the range of $f.$ These sets are called **level surfaces** because they can be visualized as surfaces in three-dimensional space, and are \"level\" in the sense that each surface corresponds to a constant value of $w.$\n\nFor this particular function, all level surfaces are spheres. For example, if we let $w = 4,$ we get\n\n$$\nx^2 +y^2+z^2 = 4,\n$$\n\nwhich is a sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/12/12de396a8460b929f656a44d701752e248180f926f22a5f5c3eeecf355e9f31a.png)\n\nFunctions with more than two input variables are sometimes called **hypersurfaces** because they can be thought of as surfaces that exist within a space whose dimension is greater than $3.$ For example, the function \n\n$$\nw = x^2+y^2+z^2\n$$\n\ncan be thought of as a sphere in four-dimensional space, and is sometimes called a **hypersphere.**\n\nAnother way of thinking about the level surfaces of this function is that they correspond to the intersection of the hypersphere $w=x^2+y^2+z^2$ and the **hyperplane** $w=c.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0682086d-c964-557f-930b-8d2c1ebfd879" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3818 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5af3abd2-cf0a-5230-8fd4-5f285e096712" :step/math-academy-id 22162 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130019}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130017 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129161 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128979 :question/id #uuid "2f8542a8-1e9e-56f8-9eae-e5b4fb592d4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5965"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5165085e-ffa9-533a-ab7c-c03304fc1489" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050005}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409920}] :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Level Surface of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "4c4da688-00e6-5efe-81b1-0c1749b7c89f" :step/math-academy-id 22163 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130018}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130018 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129162 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128980 :question/id #uuid "6a7316fa-77fe-584f-bd92-a37521371482" :question/math-academy-id "e-5966"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77be6032-ec22-5561-94c2-be289e343142" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409922}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Level Surface of a Function Passing Through a Point"} :step/id #uuid "27f830a8-2df2-594e-9bbe-3c8973052195" :step/math-academy-id 22164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130019 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129163 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128981 :question/id #uuid "397d8dee-c35f-5908-baa6-002e09dab477" :question/math-academy-id "e-5967"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5e0a37b-23ad-5e79-a175-378b88640ba4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049882}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483414}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Level Surface"} :step/id #uuid "b58904dd-f0af-56ab-937b-9eafb5f01b0f" :step/math-academy-id 22180 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130017}}] :activity/title "Level Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129326}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409924} :activity/id #uuid "7ba4c449-235c-593b-b418-e0c725f2348d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050045 :topic/id #uuid "28837c1e-d226-56e8-b958-b4375cfa8078" :topic/math-academy-id 1978} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409923 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409922 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "075b7e9b-145b-5c05-9d44-d25bd4906906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409902} {:answer/id #uuid "2ebb8400-2445-5e48-a715-3c49140534aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409903} {:answer/id #uuid "3e1d8da5-60a8-5dd3-b8c1-c0a4396407a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409904} {:answer/id #uuid "d72a7a8d-f6c0-55af-9c80-47300b8e44cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409911} {:answer/id #uuid "f658e47e-1f73-55ca-b61e-916ac3975ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409915}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409902} :answer-field/id #uuid "c684e12d-e352-5e5e-a84d-c529f9dea534" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ef96fb6f-8651-5bcd-8700-e66e3e1a4bf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-129189" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-z$ that passes through the point $P(0,-2,4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2,4) & ={2}^{2}-{0}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =4-0-4 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-z & =0 \\\\ z & ={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(0,-2,4)$ is the hyperbolic paraboloid $z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "a94aecad-6d06-5584-9ae6-ff5e4c972c6e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409927}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409924 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409920 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90d9747b-5cb9-522d-836f-d3553c78f3a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A family of planes: $x+y-{z}^{2}-\\sqrt{c}=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409906} {:answer/id #uuid "b986da89-36d0-55a1-8a48-c9bd757d18db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A family of spheres: ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}={c}^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409907} {:answer/id #uuid "c52cd181-65a2-5882-be1e-55e40ba886c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A family of hyperboloids of one sheet: ${c}^{2}{x}^{2}+{c}^{2}{y}^{2}-{c}^{2}{z}^{2}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409909} {:answer/id #uuid "d11d59ab-ca32-5bdd-988e-9f236a634db6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A family of planes: $x+y+z\\pm \\sqrt{c}=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409910} {:answer/id #uuid "e4bedb04-7179-51c0-917b-125bf66e2a73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A family of surfaces given by $z=(x+y+c)^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409912}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409910} :answer-field/id #uuid "44405f81-ea2c-5ec0-9035-361601af3ea6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "00fd1a7d-44b2-529b-bcff-08a778520166" :question/math-academy-id "q-71586" :question/problem "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)=(x+y+z)^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ (x+y+z)^{2} & =c \\\\ x+y+z & =\\pm \\sqrt{c} \\\\ x+y+z\\pm \\sqrt{c} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)$ can't be negative. Therefore, we get a family of planes:\n\n$$\nx+y+z\\pm \\sqrt{c}=0,\\,c\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bfbdcc24-f373-559b-9261-b97d80ffba78" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409925}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409925 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409921 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7161819e-ecce-539e-a7d9-dd1392ee66bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409905} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6e5c59-e63b-5d91-add0-3fc776b81f35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409908} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e2ddba-6b1f-50a3-8f82-6a0e5406e403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409913} {:answer/id #uuid "f41b80f9-1883-5d0f-a39d-6bf74d468448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-z=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409914} {:answer/id #uuid "fab3e1ad-3d44-56bd-8a1c-54fb07d88d5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409916}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409905} :answer-field/id #uuid "e10467a3-d69e-5402-aad3-4b64333f090a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "595252cd-1afe-5f16-ae97-13c476966ff1" :question/math-academy-id "q-71594" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}$ that passes through the point $P(2,\\sqrt{5},3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,\\sqrt{5},3) & =\\frac{(2)^{2}}{2(2)^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}-(3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(2,\\sqrt{5},3)$ is the surface ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=0.$"} :step/id #uuid "d981e48e-0b28-5d2c-b93e-70be17e1dbbd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409926}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409926 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338966 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "341c06af-d1ba-5272-bb61-52ad376125d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336522} {:answer/id #uuid "5700ae9e-968d-5bb9-b16c-fb2495caa581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336830} {:answer/id #uuid "726140df-5488-5e91-b878-03e0a7dc18a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337057} {:answer/id #uuid "caa01bba-d8dc-529e-84eb-7a172fdebb43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337815} {:answer/id #uuid "f1709cb8-4e61-5ab7-a2f4-a614c896f494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338145}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337815} :answer-field/id #uuid "8165f0ea-43de-5434-a42c-9e10144a7415" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e38a7de1-ef3c-50e7-9539-8728abb34f00" :question/math-academy-id "q-71597" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=z-4{x}^{2}-6{y}^{2}$ that passes through the point $P(2,1,22).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,1,22) & =22-4(2)^{2}-6(1)^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=0.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ z-4{x}^{2}-6{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ z & =4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(2,1,22)$ is the elliptic paraboloid $z=4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "f363fade-7d87-58eb-bf21-09d29aa00dc3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409923}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409927 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262977 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07f39980-6ad0-53d7-ad63-fbb34a382f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A family of elliptic paraboloids: $z={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259949} {:answer/id #uuid "1456f86c-f369-5c8a-aa73-f917e987fcd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A family of elliptic cones: ${z}^{2}={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260057} {:answer/id #uuid "64d0fc97-78ec-5077-acfa-96dd790ebb7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A family of elliptic paraboloids: $z=-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260745} {:answer/id #uuid "9b989c09-44c2-5eab-8bb7-5a6b7ceb875f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A family of planes: $z=x+3y+C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261222} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c9051c-074f-5a24-a621-b2a3449ab493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A family of hyperbolic paraboloids: $z={x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261283}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259949} :answer-field/id #uuid "653d007f-928b-501b-88fc-8c02a0e38f08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9849d588-2a95-5d11-993e-dd1a8d5d4837" :question/math-academy-id "q-129196" :question/problem "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+z+3?$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ -{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+z+3 & =c \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+c-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we let $c-3=C,$ we get a family of elliptic paraboloids:\n\n$$\nz={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+C,\\,C\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f4887751-62f7-5565-b96d-ce810c1af7bd"}] :activity/title "Level Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409901}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162952} :activity/id #uuid "6fbac718-0db1-5d0a-8eac-581af3c3ed39" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051001 :topic/id #uuid "789ccc3c-cca0-5e71-bdb6-b757b2abcfa0" :topic/math-academy-id 3001} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162952 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163262 :tutorial/content "We often want to know if there is a relationship between two random variables. For this reason, we wish to formulate the idea of a joint probability distribution.\n\nTo introduce this concept, suppose the random variable $X$ represents the number of heads obtained when a fair coin is flipped. The support of $X,$ denoted $S_X,$ consists of all possible values of $X$ and in this case is given by\n\n$$\nS_X = \\{0,1\\}.\n$$\n\nNow suppose that the random variable $Y$ represents the result of rolling a fair tetrahedral die. Then the support of $Y,$ denoted $S_Y,$ is\n\n$$\nS_Y = \\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nThe **joint support** of $X$ and $Y,$ denoted $S,$ consists of all possible pairs $(x,y)$ such that $x$ is a possible outcome for $X$ and $y$ is a possible outcome for $Y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/34/34a8b2e96a6ef41d66c441aebe08d204a0bc84b210f8de04c836907f0afd9d29.svg)\n\nNote that $S_X\\times S_Y$ is the cartesian product of $S_X$ and $S_Y.$\n\nLet's assume that the flipping of the coin and rolling of the die are independent. Then, for any $x \\in X$ and any $y \\in Y,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X=x\\:\\textrm{and}\\:Y=y) = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe **joint probability mass function** of $X$ and $Y,$ denoted $f(x,y),$ can be represented by the following table:\n\n| $f(x,y)$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ |\n\n \n\nSimilar to the case of single random variables, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = P(X=x\\:\\textrm{ and }\\:Y=y).\n$$\n\nGoing forward, we will use the shorthand notation $P(X=x, Y=y)$ to mean $P(X=x\\:\\textrm{ and }\\:Y=y).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "79b99576-5a28-5c03-bc5d-1cc472ef0795" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5348 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9628dc39-75f0-5169-a945-48c68e6e4f31" :step/math-academy-id 33477 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163076}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162982 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162063 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161863 :question/id #uuid "2d698f44-e551-58a2-883b-3a992119bd76" :question/math-academy-id "e-9126"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38398622-ca45-5eb6-a589-96aec162960a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050432}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424258}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Joint Distribution of Two Discrete Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "3e1d6fef-61c1-5a3a-805d-6a020df8bb08" :step/math-academy-id 34016 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162999}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162999 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162073 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161873 :question/id #uuid "f2789f6c-11d5-5132-91b3-be65dac4ce24" :question/math-academy-id "e-9203"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0e20c64-85be-5cb5-acde-17ffd8b6aff5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483349}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Joint Probability From a Table"} :step/id #uuid "b093ac87-86c2-5281-860f-df1321b270d4" :step/math-academy-id 34339 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163000}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163000 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162074 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161874 :question/id #uuid "01b86f31-f31e-567d-b798-fc0f10a4d0b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9204"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e247e2c9-3e47-5cf4-be8d-f1f22bb30b39" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424257}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Joint Probability Containing Inequalities From a Table"} :step/id #uuid "657c560d-1a7e-5600-8883-4eaa35b157cd" :step/math-academy-id 34343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163076 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163328 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with supports $S_X$ and $S_Y$, respectively. For a function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=P(X=x, Y=y)\n$$\n\n \n\nto be a valid joint probability mass function with joint support $S=S_X\\times S_Y,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0 \\leq f(x,y) \\leq 1$ for all $(x,y) \\in S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\sum\\limits_{(x,y) \\in S} f(x,y)= 1$\n\n- $P\\left((X,Y) \\in A \\right) = \\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{(x,y) \\in A} f(x,y),$ where $A$ is a subset of $S.$\n\nThere is some nice intuition to these conditions.\n\n- The condition $0 \\leq f(x,y) \\leq 1$ for all $(x,y)\\in S$ states that any possible outcome for $(X,Y)$ must have a probability between $0$ and $1.$\n\n- The condition $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{(x,y) \\in S} f(x,y) = 1$ states that the sum of all the probabilities over all possible values of $X$ and $Y$ must add up to $1.$\n\n- The third condition states that we compute the probability of an event $A$ by adding up the probabilities associated with $A.$\n\nThe joint probability mass function of a discrete random variable with finite support can be represented using a table, as shown below.\n\n|  | $Y=y_1$ | $Y=y_2$ | $\\ldots$ | $Y=y_n$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=x_1$ | $f(x_1,y_1)$ | $f(x_1,y_2)$ | $\\ldots$ | $f(x_1,y_n)$ |\n| $X=x_2$ | $f(x_2,y_1)$ | $f(x_2,y_2)$ | $\\ldots$ | $f(x_2,y_n)$ |\n| $\\ldots$ | $\\ldots$ | $\\ldots$ | $\\ldots$ | $\\ldots$ |\n| $X=x_k$ | $f(x_k,y_1)$ | $f(x_k,y_2)$ | $\\ldots$ | $f(x_k,y_n)$ |\n\nIn the case of two random variables, the joint distribution is sometimes called a **bivariate distribution**.\nHowever, the concept generalizes to any number of random variables, giving a **multivariate distribution**." :tutorial/id #uuid "5f5858f9-f1f3-5d98-b436-9ffd034a3e9d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6284 :tutorial/title "Bivariate and Multivariate Distributions"} :step/id #uuid "2636297d-b114-590c-9454-08ebab970ce2" :step/math-academy-id 38084 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162982}}] :activity/title "Joint Distributions for Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162162}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423193} :activity/id #uuid "364d0a99-928c-55de-b781-b3c99a34098f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051001 :topic/id #uuid "789ccc3c-cca0-5e71-bdb6-b757b2abcfa0" :topic/math-academy-id 3001} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423191 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423190 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "172b0d28-9053-5c75-82a3-9176b2177f7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423184} {:answer/id #uuid "19dfeb7e-c8d5-5045-9993-de85d2a62535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423185} {:answer/id #uuid "311d7f39-80c9-5bd3-b7a2-7108330abf41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423186} {:answer/id #uuid "80910aba-a6f7-5cea-a000-b98b3f7a7db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423187} {:answer/id #uuid "9c647d4a-3f89-51f3-80fe-0c630dad6a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423188}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423184} :answer-field/id #uuid "80684957-1816-5bbf-b499-0054dfab3f98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d9a0e99d-ee2e-54a9-9f59-8e7f0b18bda7" :question/math-academy-id "q-110966" :question/problem "|  | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.01$ | $0.05$ | $0.025$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.025$ | $0.01$ | $0.01$ | $0.025$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.01$ | $0.05$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.01$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.1$ | $0.025$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n\nCalculate $P(Y-X=2)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y-X=2) & =P(X=0,Y=2)+P(X=1,Y=3) \\\\  & +P(X=2,Y=4)+P(X=3,Y=5) \\\\  & =f(0,2)+f(1,3)+f(2,4)+f(3,5) \\\\  & =0.01+0.01+0.1+0.1 \\\\  & =0.22.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3c65c513-af22-54cc-8e3c-607c59a2146a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423192 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "039b80b2-527f-56cc-a9bc-ff3bc90ac111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244338} {:answer/id #uuid "27f7a13e-be49-5079-b844-a4c3415b2fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244665} {:answer/id #uuid "adbb15ed-5e20-5e15-ac70-ff60a0a5d46d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245865} {:answer/id #uuid "b6826898-2df3-5edc-9c17-76d4e6512dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245945} {:answer/id #uuid "b6eb299c-829c-5764-a80a-06bf21a8c82e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245948}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245945} :answer-field/id #uuid "89831f9f-6346-59a1-a3f3-bf51f497aaa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "89e9e61a-c7f2-587b-936c-fdec6e3defa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-109687" :question/problem "|  | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.12$ | $0.23$ |\n\nCalculate $P(X+Y=1)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y=1) & =P(X=0,Y=1)+P(X=1,Y=0) \\\\  & =f(0,1)+f(1,0) \\\\  & =0.25+0.2 \\\\  & =0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6721940f-5f05-5fad-bcea-05d07a41f55c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423191}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423193 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fd153f7-654f-5ed4-b9fe-3b1731a70ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241460} {:answer/id #uuid "5fa1370c-c82b-5fe3-ad6e-a777ec65e824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242039} {:answer/id #uuid "a54e62bf-fa8c-5810-a8eb-8f31624180ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242635} {:answer/id #uuid "c272c385-4c5e-5735-b854-1a68b031613e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242885} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8dd4bf-32b3-55ab-8c67-2219401fd8d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243407}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242635} :answer-field/id #uuid "5497cc27-067a-5c7c-b637-6c6d86e6b1e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "10ef8c30-25b6-5a94-b4c4-25e4aa370204" :question/math-academy-id "q-109336" :question/problem "|  | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCompute $P(X=2\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,Y\\le 4)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,Y\\le 4) & =P((X,Y)\\in \\{(2,2),(2,4)\\}) \\\\  & =P((X,Y)=(2,2))+P((X,Y)=(2,4)) \\\\  & =f(2,2)+f(2,4) \\\\  & =0.05+0.15 \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bb98f552-49b6-5d50-9a85-6fcbe8252a94" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423192}}] :activity/title "Joint Distributions for Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423183}]}
